s/  
- **Publisher/Author:** Joslyn Art Museum (event/lecture page; no individual 
author named)  
- **Date:** Not stated (event page; likely mid‑2010s, but no explicit date on 
the snippet)  
- **Source type:** Museum/exhibition event description (institutional webpage)  
- **Triggered by query:** `"Ring of Nestor gold ring Mycenaean Pylos discovery 
archaeologists -site:wikipedia.org"`  
- **Relevance:** High. The page concerns the “Griffin Warrior” grave at Pylos 
and explicitly mentions “gold rings” in that burial, which appear to be the same
context as the ring later popularly associated with “Nestor.” It supports 
identification of the ring as a Mycenaean/Minoan-style gold signet ring and 
links it to the Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos.  
- **Content / faithful summary:**  
  - The page announces/advertises a lecture or event titled “The Lord of the 
Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos.” It is hosted by the Joslyn Art 
Museum. [1]  
  - It notes that major archaeological discoveries at Pylos include:  
    - The grave of the Griffin Warrior at Pylos.  
    - Two new Mycenaean tholos tombs at Pylos.  
    - Other previously unknown Greek sites. [1]  
  - It emphasizes that a rich assemblage of artifacts was found in the Griffin 
Warrior’s grave, including gold signet rings, and refers to “gold rings” as 
particularly significant items. The exact wording of the snippet indicates that 
these gold rings are central to the lecture topic, though the page as indexed 
focuses more on the lecture announcement than detailed description. [1]  
  - By context, it associates the gold rings with Mycenaean Pylos and the 
Griffin Warrior burial; the rings are described as high-status objects of 
significant artistic and archaeological importance. [1]

