- **Date:** 27 October 2015  
- **Source type:** Online archaeology news/article (specialist magazine site)  
- **Triggered by query:** `"Ring of Nestor gold ring Mycenaean Pylos discovery 
archaeologists -site:wikipedia.org"`  
- **Relevance:** High. This article reports on the Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos
and explicitly describes one of the gold rings with a Cretan bull‑jumping scene,
likely the ring later dubbed the “Ring of Nestor.” It gives physical description
and discovery context (Pylos, excavation team, date).  
- **Content / faithful summary:**  
  - The article reports on “rare archaeological finds in Pylos,” related to the 
discovery of an undisturbed shaft grave, later dubbed the Griffin Warrior’s 
tomb, near the so‑called Palace of Nestor at Pylos in southwestern Greece. [2]  
  - It notes that the grave dates to the Late Bronze Age / Mycenaean period and 
is associated with the early phases of Mycenaean civilization. [2]  
  - The article states that the grave contained an unusually rich assemblage of 
grave goods, including bronze weapons, jewelry, and gold objects. [2]  
  - Among these artifacts were four solid gold signet rings. The article 
emphasizes that this number of gold rings in a single grave is greater than that
found with any other known single Mycenaean burial at that time. [2]  
  - It describes one of the gold rings as decorated with a Cretan bull‑jumping 
scene (a motif characteristic of Minoan iconography), indicating that the ring’s
imagery is linked to Cretan/Minoan ritual or athletic performance. [2]  
  - The article credits the discovery to an excavation team working at Pylos, 
connected with the University of Cincinnati and the Greek Archaeological 
Service, and notes that these finds were made during a specific excavation 
season in the mid‑2010s (2015). [2]  
  - It indicates that the tomb and its contents were discovered near the Palace 
of Nestor, confirming the geographic link between the Griffin Warrior grave and 
the broader Pylos/Nestor archaeological complex. [2]

