and context for the discovery.  
- **Content / faithful summary:**  
  - The article highlights the work of University of Cincinnati archaeologists 
at Pylos in Greece. [3]  
  - It notes that UC archaeologist Carl Blegen first discovered the Mycenaean 
ruins at Pylos in 1939, including the remains of what came to be known as the 
Palace of Nestor. [3]  
  - The feature then turns to more recent excavations by UC archaeologists 
Sharon Stocker and Jack Davis, who led an excavation team that uncovered the 
“Griffin Warrior” grave near the Palace of Nestor. [3]  
  - It describes that the Griffin Warrior’s grave contained a wealth of 
artifacts, including bronze weapons, jewelry and multiple gold signet rings, 
which are described as elaborately engraved. [3]  
  - The article emphasizes that among the gold rings there are scenes in a 
Minoan style, including bull‑leaping and other ritual or mythological imagery, 
highlighting the cultural connections between the Mycenaean mainland and Crete. 
[3]  
  - It dates the discovery of the Griffin Warrior grave to the 2015 excavation 
season, explaining that the tomb had remained undisturbed for more than 3,000 
years. [3]  
  - It credits the discovery to the excavation team directed by UC 
archaeologists Stocker and Davis, conducted under the auspices of the Greek 
Ministry of Culture and the Greek Archaeological Service. [3]  
  - It notes that the artifacts, including the gold rings, were conserved and 
studied under controlled conditions in Greece, and that they are considered some
of the most spectacular finds in Aegean archaeology in recent decades. [3]

