discussion of Minoan sphragistic traditions, tracing the development of signet 
rings, their iconography, and their role in royal and religious contexts. In the
broader table of contents, Evans explores topics such as hunting scenes, animal 
types, the evolution of lentoid seal designs, clay seal impressions from 
Knossos, and the association of signet rings with Linear B administrative 
practices. The Royal Signet-Ring chapter draws on legendary and literary 
traditions surrounding Minos’s ring and his temple tomb, and likely references 
famous rings including the Ring of Nestor as comparative examples of high-status
signet rings. While not exclusively about the Ring of Nestor, the chapter offers
key contextual information on the typology, function, and symbolic meaning of 
Minoan–Mycenaean signet rings, supporting encyclopedic statements about the Ring
