### 1. A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity” 
(Aegis 18, 2020)

- **Title**: (2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) *Current Approaches and New Perspectives in
Aegean Iconography*, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223–252.
- **URL**: 
https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A_G_Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_
the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F_Blakolmer_ed_Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspect
ives_in_Aegean_Iconography_Aegis_18_2020_pp_223_252
- **Domain**: academia.edu
- **Source type**: Academic publication / scholarly article hosted on 
Academia.edu.
- **Date**: 2020 (article; specific publication date not given in page text).
- **Triggered by query**: `"Ring of Nestor" signet ring Evans 1925 authenticity 
OJA 17 1-13`
- **Raw / summarized content**:
  - Abstract: The study focuses on the so‑called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 
277), documenting its authenticity after years of research.[1]
  - It traces earlier scholarly tendencies in the 1920s to label several 
Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings, including the Ring of Nestor and Ring of Minos, as 
forgeries, largely due to obscure or “legendary” find circumstances.[1]
  - Recent discoveries of comparable iconographic subjects (wall paintings, gold
rings, figurines, ivories) and detailed study of technique and craftsmanship 
have shifted opinion strongly in favour of the rings’ authenticity, situating 
them among unique signet rings of the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age 
(16th–15th century BC).[1]
  - The key pictorial unit discussed is Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon,” 
depicted as a guardian of the “tree of the world.” Vlachopoulos reidentifies 
this motif as a scorpion, arguing that the scorpion functioned as a major 
apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography and offers a coherent 
interpretation of the ring’s imagery.[1]
- **Relevance to research goal**:
  - Provides modern, detailed analysis of the Ring of Nestor’s iconography and 
authenticity debates.
  - Offers specific measurements (3.29 × 2.18 cm, fifth-largest Aegean signet 
ring) and dating to the 16th–15th century BC.[1]
  - Highly relevant for sections on description, dating, cultural attribution, 
and scholarly significance.
- **Compliance notes**:
  - Domain-level inspection: academia.edu is an academic hosting platform, not a
Wikipedia mirror or scraper.
  - No links here to wikipedia.org or obvious mirror domains.
  - Suitable as a non‑Wikipedia source in the final article’s References list.

