  "documents": [
    {"local_ref": "d1", "document_summary": "Scholarly article by A. G. 
Vlachopoulos examining the Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277). It reviews early 
20th-century tendencies to consider certain Creto-Mycenaean gold rings, 
including the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos, as forgeries because of 
unclear or legendary find circumstances. Drawing on newly discovered comparanda 
in wall paintings, gold rings, figurines, and ivories, along with detailed 
analysis of technique and craftsmanship, the study argues strongly for the 
authenticity of these rings and situates the Ring of Nestor among major signet 
rings of the early Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC). The paper provides 
specific measurements for the ring and notes it is among the largest Aegean 
signet rings. Central to the discussion is the main pictorial unit: Evans’s 
so-called watch-dog or dragon guarding a tree, reinterpreted by Vlachopoulos as 
a scorpion serving as an apotropaic symbol and guardian of the ‘tree of the 
world’, with implications for understanding Bronze Age Aegean iconography.", 
"source_ref": "s1"},
    {"local_ref": "d2", "document_summary": "Peer-reviewed journal article in 
the Journal of Ancient Egyptian Interconnections discussing the so-called Nestor
Ring. It recounts that the ring was found in 1924 and initially accepted as 
authentic by Arthur Evans, but later many scholars condemned it as a forgery. 
The article focuses on the ring’s Egyptian iconography and characterizes it as 
‘pseudo-Minoan’, engaging with the authenticity debate by examining stylistic 
and iconographic links between the ring and Egyptian motifs.", "source_ref": 
"s2"},
    {"local_ref": "d3", "document_summary": "Digitized page from Arthur J. 
Evans’s 1925 publication on a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê in Boeotia, including the section titled “The ring of 
Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world.” Evans introduces the Ring of 
Nestor as part of a Boeotian treasure and describes its discovery and 
acquisition history. He provides an interpretative reading of the ring’s 
iconography, including scenes related to the Minoan afterlife, a tree with a 
guardian creature, and altars, supported by illustrations and collotype plates. 
The page represents primary early-20th-century documentation of the ring’s 
context and imagery.", "source_ref": "s3"},
    {"local_ref": "d4", "document_summary": "Academic article by Theodore 
Eliopoulos, published in 2013 in the Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene
e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente, offering observations on the iconography 
of the Ring of Nestor. The study situates the ring within wider issues of Aegean
forgeries and authenticity, emphasizing that iconographic analysis is crucial 
alongside technical studies. It discusses features such as incurved altars 
depicted on the ring and compares them with similar motifs elsewhere, arguing 
that such iconographic parallels support the ring’s authenticity. The article 
notes the longstanding scholarly debate over the genuineness of the Ring of 
Nestor and the Ring of Minos and advocates more systematic study of rings and 
unprovenanced finger rings.", "source_ref": "s4"},
    {"local_ref": "d5", "document_summary": "HathiTrust catalog record for 
Arthur Evans’s 1925 work titled “The ring of Nestor; a glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia.” The record provides bibliographic metadata including author, 
title, and publication information and serves as a stable reference entry for 
the original publication.", "source_ref": "s5"},
    {"local_ref": "d6", "document_summary": "Feature article in the 
archaeology/history magazine The Past describing the discovery and contents of 
the Griffin Warrior tomb near Pylos, found in 2015. It outlines the rich 
assemblage of grave goods, including gold signet rings and weapons. The article 
mentions that one gold signet ring from Tholos IV and the only other from this 
area is the famous Ring of Nestor, purchased by Arthur Evans, and uses this to 
connect the Griffin Warrior material to broader Mycenaean and Minoan glyptic 
traditions. It situates the Ring of Nestor within the wider archaeological and 
cultural context of Mycenaean palatial society and recent discoveries.", 
"source_ref": "s6"},
    {"local_ref": "d7", "document_summary": "Academia.edu entry for the 
scholarly article “The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos,” 
originally published in Hesperia. The visible snippet indicates that the article
discusses the Griffin Warrior’s gold rings and compares them with the Ring of 
Nestor, citing Evans 1925 (p. 47, fig. 42). It notes that one of the Griffin 
Warrior rings (Ring 2) appears to support the authenticity of both the Ring of 
Nestor and another ring by sharing small iconographic and technical details, 
such as a woody motif. The article places the Ring of Nestor within a network of
Minoan-style signet rings and uses comparative evidence from securely 
provenanced finds to argue for authenticity.", "source_ref": "s7"},
    {"local_ref": "d8", "document_summary": "KU ScholarWorks PDF of the volume 
“Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography” (Aegis 18), 
edited by F. Blakolmer. The PDF includes references to Aegean glyptic studies, 
among them the article “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity” and 
discussions citing the Ring of Nestor and related works such as Pini 1999. The 
document serves as an institutional host for the volume containing 
Vlachopoulos’s detailed study of the Ring of Nestor and broader iconographic 
analyses.", "source_ref": "s8"}
  ],
  "statements": [
