  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "The Griffin Warrior project site describes the 
discovery and excavation of the Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor 
at Pylos by a University of Cincinnati team in May 2015. It states that the tomb
dates to the Late Helladic IIA period and contained the burial of a single elite
male accompanied by a rich assemblage of grave goods. These included weapons, 
seal stones, jewelry, and multiple gold signet rings decorated with Minoan 
ritual scenes. The site outlines the excavation process, situates the tomb 
within the Bronze Age landscape of Pylos, and emphasizes the importance of the 
finds for understanding Mycenaean–Minoan interaction and elite identity. The 
description of the rings and their iconography is used to illustrate broader 
patterns of Aegean gold signet rings and demonstrates thorough archaeological 
reporting with clear contextualization and citation practices.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The Hesperia publication page for \"The Lord of the 
Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos\" summarizes a detailed scholarly 
article on the Griffin Warrior tomb excavated near Pylos. It explains that the 
article reports the discovery and excavation of a large stone-built tomb dating 
to the Late Helladic IIA period, containing a single elite male burial with rich
grave goods, especially four gold signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual 
scenes. The article covers the tomb’s stratigraphy and dating, provides detailed
descriptions of the gold rings including their iconography and stylistic 
parallels, and discusses their implications for Mycenaean–Minoan cultural 
interaction and elite ideology. The page notes that the full article includes 
extensive footnotes and references, exemplifying a structure in which 
descriptive and interpretive statements are thoroughly supported by citations.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The Academia.edu entry provides access to the full 
text of the article \"The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos\"
by Jack L. Davis and colleagues. Its abstract states that in May 2015 a 
University of Cincinnati team discovered a large stone-built tomb of Late 
Helladic IIA date near Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos on the first day of the 
