{"query": {"id": "20260628151242196940_5b4863a0", "handle": "q2", "text": "Ring 
of Nestor gold ring Mycenaean Pylos discovery archaeologists", "importance": 
"core"}, "items": [{"id": "20260628151609973797_c0e056f4", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Joslyn Art Museum announces a lecture titled “The Lord of the Gold 
Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos,” highlighting gold signet rings from the 
Griffin Warrior’s grave as central objects of discussion.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607702444_8bd4c882"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628151610027244_dd6772ed", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Archaeology.wiki article reports an undisturbed shaft grave found near the 
so‑called Palace of Nestor at Pylos in southwestern Greece, later identified as 
the Griffin Warrior’s tomb.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607724111_65006dd7"], 
"citations": [10]}, {"id": "20260628151610082394_e7999d31", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Among the grave goods were four solid gold signet rings; the article 
notes that no other known single Mycenaean burial had yielded so many gold 
rings.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607724111_65006dd7"], "citations": [10]}, 
{"id": "20260628151610137951_d350283b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "One of the 
gold rings is described as decorated with a Cretan bull‑jumping scene 
characteristic of Minoan iconography.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607724111_65006dd7"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628151610195596_41445e54", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Archaeology.wiki article credits the discovery to an excavation team linked to 
the University of Cincinnati and the Greek Archaeological Service during the 
2015 excavation season.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607724111_65006dd7"], 
"citations": [10]}, {"id": "20260628151610250929_cdccbfce", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "University of Cincinnati Magazine recounts that Carl Blegen first 
discovered Mycenaean ruins at Pylos, including what became known as the Palace 
of Nestor, in 1939.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607747572_5d40737b"], 
"citations": [11]}, {"id": "20260628151610305490_70192603", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The same feature states that archaeologists Sharon Stocker and Jack 
Davis led the excavation that uncovered the Griffin Warrior’s grave near the 
Palace of Nestor in 2015.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607747572_5d40737b"], 
"citations": [11]}, {"id": "20260628151610366873_faa67ec2", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The article notes that the Griffin Warrior’s grave contained 
elaborately engraved gold signet rings alongside bronze weapons and jewelry, 
with scenes in a Minoan style including bull‑leaping.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607747572_5d40737b"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628151610428021_adfd13a5", "type": "Evidence", "text": "According to the 
UC Magazine feature, the excavation was conducted under the auspices of the 
Greek Ministry of Culture and the Greek Archaeological Service, and the 
artifacts were conserved and studied in Greece.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607747572_5d40737b"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628151610501203_bb1a7b7b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Academia.edu 
paper describes four signet rings among the gold artifacts from the Griffin 
Warrior’s grave, decorated with Minoan ritual scenes such as bull‑leaping and 
goddess figures.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607771821_88154e17"], "citations":
[12]}, {"id": "20260628151610561985_6c61dcb9", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
paper situates the burial and its gold rings in the Late Helladic I–II period 
and argues that their iconography reflects early Mycenaean elites’ appropriation
of Minoan symbols and artistic styles.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607771821_88154e17"], "citations": [12]}, {"id": 
"20260628151610616650_46f3560e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The ResearchGate 
abstract likewise notes that four signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual 
scenes were among the gold artifacts from the Griffin Warrior’s grave.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151607795021_bc51af61"], "citations": [13]}, {"id": 
"20260628151610672095_34a0d0a8", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Archaeology 
magazine feature recounts that Stocker and Davis discovered an undisturbed 
Bronze Age grave near the Palace of Nestor in 2015, later called the Griffin 
Warrior’s tomb.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607819814_d8910039"], "citations": 
[14]}, {"id": "20260628151610729596_c62895b3", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
feature dates the burial to the early Mycenaean period in the 15th century BCE 
and identifies the interred individual as an elite male warrior.", "source_ids":
["20260628151607819814_d8910039"], "citations": [14]}, {"id": 
"20260628151610793096_df44b40e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The article 
describes four intricately carved gold signet rings among the grave goods and 
characterizes them as among the finest known examples.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607819814_d8910039"], "citations": [14]}, {"id": 
"20260628151610856132_b322a70f", "type": "Evidence", "text": "These rings bear 
imagery such as bull‑leaping and complex ritual scenes rendered in a Minoan 
artistic style, which the article interprets as evidence for close connections 
with Crete.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607819814_d8910039"], "citations": 
[14]}, {"id": "20260628151610915578_0b151ae7", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
National Archaeological Museum’s exhibition description states that the show 
“Pylos of Nestor. A Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed” presents finds from the 
Mycenaean palace complex at Pylos, illustrating political organization, 
administration, religion, and daily life.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607845429_b9e6f5ee"], "citations": [15]}, {"id": 
"20260628151610970649_150781ae", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The exhibition 
page notes that objects from Pylos, including artifacts from recent excavations,
are displayed and that they come from the collections of the National 
Archaeological Museum and associated institutions.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607845429_b9e6f5ee"], "citations": [15]}, {"id": 
"20260628151611038011_773b617c", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Griffin 
Warrior Project website describes the grave as an undisturbed shaft tomb dating 
to the early Mycenaean period, discovered near the Palace of Nestor by a team 
associated with the University of Cincinnati under Greek supervision.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151607868722_8563b539"], "citations": [16]}, {"id": 
"20260628151611094841_1e85ed9f", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The site lists 
grave goods including bronze weapons, armor elements, jewelry, stone seals, and 
finely engraved gold signet rings, and emphasizes the exceptional preservation 
and importance of the find.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607868722_8563b539"], 
"citations": [16]}, {"id": "20260628151611155349_542dac5f", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The Griffin Warrior Project states that the artifacts, including the 
gold rings, are curated and studied under the authority of the Greek state in 
collaboration with the University of Cincinnati team.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607868722_8563b539"], "citations": [16]}, {"id": 
"20260628151658673600_1a613919", "type": "Claim", "text": "The so‑called Griffin
Warrior’s grave is an undisturbed Bronze Age shaft tomb near the Palace of 
Nestor at Mycenaean Pylos in southwestern Greece, dating to the early Mycenaean 
/ Late Helladic I–II period in the 15th century BCE and belonging to an elite 
male warrior.", "source_ids": ["20260628151607724111_65006dd7", 
"20260628151607771821_88154e17", "20260628151607819814_d8910039", 
"20260628151607868722_8563b539"], "citations": [10, 12, 14, 16]}, {"id": 
"20260628151658864519_82b278f4", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Griffin Warrior’s
grave was discovered in 2015 near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos by an excavation
team led by archaeologists Sharon Stocker and Jack Davis, associated with the 
University of Cincinnati and working under the authority of the Greek Ministry 
of Culture and the Greek Archaeological Service.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607724111_65006dd7", "20260628151607747572_5d40737b", 
"20260628151607819814_d8910039", "20260628151607868722_8563b539"], "citations": 
[10, 11, 14, 16]}, {"id": "20260628151659076521_6c476eb2", "type": "Claim", 
"text": "Among the grave goods in the Griffin Warrior’s tomb were four solid 
gold signet rings, elaborately and finely engraved, which are described as among
the finest known examples and represent an unprecedented number of gold rings 
for a single known Mycenaean burial.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607724111_65006dd7", "20260628151607747572_5d40737b", 
"20260628151607819814_d8910039", "20260628151607868722_8563b539"], "citations": 
[10, 11, 14, 16]}, {"id": "20260628151659258405_b009e324", "type": "Claim", 
"text": "The gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior’s grave bear 
Minoan‑style imagery, including bull‑leaping scenes, goddess figures, and 
complex ritual or mythological scenes, and at least one ring shows a Cretan 
bull‑jumping scene characteristic of Minoan iconography.", "source_ids": 
["20260628151607724111_65006dd7", "20260628151607747572_5d40737b", 
"20260628151607771821_88154e17", "20260628151607795021_bc51af61", 
"20260628151607819814_d8910039"], "citations": [10, 11, 12, 13, 14]}, {"id": 
"20260628151659471534_113d1010", "type": "Claim", "text": "Scholars interpret 
the Minoan‑style iconography on the Griffin Warrior’s gold signet rings as 
evidence of close cultural and possibly political connections between early 
Mycenaean elites at Pylos and Minoan Crete, and as part of the formation of 
Mycenaean identity and elite power in the Late Helladic I–II period.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151607702444_8bd4c882", "20260628151607747572_5d40737b",
"20260628151607771821_88154e17", "20260628151607795021_bc51af61", 
"20260628151607819814_d8910039"], "citations": [9, 11, 12, 13, 14]}, {"id": 
"20260628151659673390_1d23109b", "type": "Claim", "text": "The artifacts from 
the Griffin Warrior’s grave, including the gold rings, are curated, conserved, 
and studied in Greece under the authority of Greek state institutions, and some 
artifacts from Pylos are presented in exhibitions such as the National 
Archaeological Museum’s “Pylos of Nestor. A Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed.”", 
"source_ids": ["20260628151607747572_5d40737b", "20260628151607845429_b9e6f5ee",
